    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests pass).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephones in `OwnerTelephoneNormalizationAdvice.java` \u2014 the advice that already normalizes owner telephones to E.164 and rejects invalid ones with 400.\n\n**What I added** (one file touched):\n- A constant `NATIONAL_DIGITS = Map.of(\"61\", 9, \"1\", 10)` \u2014 the required national-number digit count per country code.\n- A small, cohesive `checkNationalLength(String)` method that strips the leading `+`, and for a known country-code prefix rejects the number with the existing `InvalidTelephoneException` (\u2192 HTTP 400) when the national part isn't the required length. Unknown country codes are left to the existing generic 8\u201315 rule.\n- One line in `afterBodyRead` calling the new check right after normalization.\n\n**Why this shape** \u2014 to keep structural impact low per the cost model:\n- I called the check from `afterBodyRead` (cyclomatic complexity 1) rather than growing `toE164` (CC 5), so the edited-method's CC multiplier stays minimal.\n- The new behavior is a self-contained unit with its own low complexity, so it doesn't inflate any existing method or the surrounding class context.\n- Only one file changed.\n\nThe `\"61\"`/`\"1\"` prefixes are disjoint (an Australian `61\u2026` number never starts with `1`), so the map iteration order is irrelevant. The valid fixtures still pass: a `+61` number with 9 national digits (11 total) and a `+1` number with 10 national digits (11 total) are both accepted, while wrong lengths like `+61 123` are rejected with 400.",
